Q: Pixi Game Embedded in a Page Using Shadow DOM Forces the Page to Refresh on iOS A: <p>The fix to Problem 1 is easy. If you need to separate the Javascript logically from each-other, then you can wrap a function around them, like</p>
<pre><code>if (typeof functions === &quot;undefined&quot;) {
    var functions = [];
}
functions.push(function() {
    const lang = &quot;en&quot;;
    //...
});
functions[functions.length - 1]();
</code></pre>
<p>and you can do this as many times as you wish, now your inner constants are guaranteed not to conflict with each-other.</p>
<p>As about the second problem that you had, it is formulated in a very vague manner. You will need to experiment and see what the actual problem is. See whether a resize causes it, benchmark memory usage, etc. etc. And then, when you have more solid understanding of what the issue may be, try to solve it and perhaps ask a new question here if needed.</p>

Q: Silent integer overflow: std::stoi A: <p>If you just want to convert a string to an integer, you can use <code>std::from_chars</code>. Since C++23, <code>std::from_chars</code> is constexpr and the whole constexpr expression can catch signed integer overflow at compile time.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;charconv&gt;
#include &lt;cstddef&gt;
#include &lt;cstdio&gt;

constexpr char buf[4096] = &quot;4096&quot;;
constexpr int bufsize = 4;

constexpr size_t buggy = []() -&gt; size_t {
    int val;
    std::from_chars(buf, buf + bufsize, val);
    return val * 1024 * 1024; // compiler error: overflow in constant expression
}();

constexpr size_t fixed = []() -&gt; size_t {
    unsigned long long val;
    std::from_chars(buf, buf + bufsize, val);
    return val * 1024ULL * 1024ULL;
}();

int main() {
    printf(&quot;buggy = %zu\n&quot;, buggy);
    printf(&quot;fixed = %zu\n&quot;, fixed);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Compile it with <code>g++ -std=c++23</code></p>

Q: Can you create inset drop-shadows in CSS? A: <p><strong>Is this what you're looking for?</strong></p>
<pre><code>filter: drop-shadow(0px 0px 20px red)
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>img{
   cursor: pointer;
   filter: drop-shadow(12px 10px 4px rgba(0,0,20,0.18));
   transition: 0.3s ease-out;
}
img:hover{
   transform: translate(4px, 4px);
   filter: drop-shadow(4px 4px 1px rgba(0,0,20,0.5))
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;img src="https://interactive-examples.mdn.mozilla.net/media/examples/firefox-logo.svg" width=100/&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: HTML adding a sticky menu bar to a scrolling text dialog - text appears above bar A: <p>I propose that you move the padding to the dialog content and then use a border
at the top and the sides of your banner, to be sure to hide anything passing
behind it.</p>
<p>You can also add a smooth gradient to slowly hide the text passing underneath the
banner. I first wanted to do this with a box shadow on the banner, but it wasn't
strong enough. Perhaps you could combine several box shadows to make it more opaque
but I finally used a pseudo-element placed below the banner.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>:root {
  --dialog-padding: 1rem;
  --dialog-bg-color: white;
}

dialog {
  padding: 0; /* Will be set on the child .dialog-content element. */
  background-color: var(--dialog-bg-color);
}

#mydialog {
  width: 240px; /* Specific to this dialog only. */
}

.dialog-banner {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  background-color: #c0c0c0;
  /* Set same border width as the dialog padding. */
  border: var(--dialog-padding) solid var(--dialog-bg-color);
  /* But not below the dialog banner. */
  border-bottom: none;
  padding: .25em;
}

/* Instead of a box-shadow, let's create a gradient to slowly
   hide the text which will scroll up beneath it. */
.dialog-banner::after {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 1.5em;
  background: linear-gradient(0deg, transparent 0%, var(--dialog-bg-color) 100%);
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%; /* Put it at the bottom of the banner. */
  left: 0;
}

.dialog-content {
  padding: var(--dialog-padding);
}

#mydialog .dialog-content {
  font-size: 32px; /* Specific to this dialog only. */
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button commandfor="mydialog" command="show-modal"&gt;dialog&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;dialog id="mydialog" closedby="any"&gt;
  &lt;div class="dialog-banner"&gt;
    File  Edit  Tools  Help
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="dialog-content"&gt;
    A long screed which will scroll and scroll and scroll but should not scroll
    behind the banner. At least, that's the hope.
&lt;/dialog&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Text overflowing the sides of its container div A: <p>The problem is that you have a very long word. You can do as they said, with <code>word-wrap: break-word;</code>, or you can use <code>text-overflow: ellipsis;</code> to hide the overflowing text, rendering <code>...</code> at the end of it, i.e:</p>
<pre><code>p {
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Where:</p>
<p><code>text-overflow: clip|ellipsis|string;</code></p>
<p><a href="http://www.w3schools.com/cssref/css3_pr_text-overflow.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">More info</a></p>
